---
import DocsLayout from "@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import DocPageShell from "@/components/DocPageShell.astro";
import Callout from "@/components/Callout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "@/components/CodeBlock.astro";
---
<DocsLayout
	title="Compiling to an executable — ayjnt docs"
	description="ayjnt compile packs agents, UIs, cli.ts, host tools, the Bun runtime and workerd into one file. Size, flags, how it works, where state lives, and what doesn't survive."
>
	<DocPageShell
		slug="guides/compile"
		lede="ayjnt compile produces a single self-contained executable: your agents, their bundled UIs, cli.ts, host tools, the Bun runtime, and workerd. Running it needs no Bun, no node_modules, and no wrangler."
	>
		<h2>The happy path</h2>
		<CodeBlock
			lang="sh"
			code={`$ ayjnt compile
[ayjnt] embedding workerd (103MB)
[ayjnt] compiling → notes
✓ ayjnt: notes (170MB)
  cli.ts runs in the foreground; workerd stops when it returns.
  1 host tool file(s) embedded — these work here but cannot be deployed.

$ ./notes add "hello from a binary"
added 0c4f1b7e-…`}
		/>
		<p>
			The output is a normal executable. Copy it to another machine of the
			same platform and it runs — there is nothing to install alongside it.
		</p>

		<h2>Size, stated plainly</h2>
		<p>
			The default binary is <strong>~170MB</strong>: roughly 57MB of Bun,
			103MB of workerd, and your app code. That is the honest cost of
			shipping two runtimes in one file, and it isn't going to shrink much
			— both halves are real runtimes.
		</p>
		<p>
			<code>--no-embed-workerd</code> brings it down to about 67MB, but the
			binary then needs a workerd on the host and{" "}
			<code>AYJNT_WORKERD_PATH</code> pointing at it. That's a reasonable
			trade for internal tooling on machines you control, and a bad one for
			anything you hand to someone else — the whole point of the default is
			that there is nothing to install.
		</p>

		<h2>Flags</h2>
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr>
					<th>Flag</th>
					<th>Effect</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr>
					<td>
						<code>--outfile &lt;path&gt;</code>
					</td>
					<td>
						Output path. Default{" "}
						<code>./&lt;worker-name&gt;</code>, taken from the
						generated wrangler config.
					</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>
						<code>--target &lt;bun-target&gt;</code>
					</td>
					<td>
						Bun compile target, e.g. <code>bun-linux-x64</code>. See
						cross-compiling in the limitations below.
					</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>
						<code>--no-embed-workerd</code>
					</td>
					<td>
						Leave workerd out (~67MB). Needs{" "}
						<code>AYJNT_WORKERD_PATH</code> at runtime.
					</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>
						<code>--bytecode</code>
					</td>
					<td>Precompile to bytecode for faster startup.</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>
						<code>--minify</code>
					</td>
					<td>Minify the embedded JavaScript.</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>
						<code>--cwd &lt;path&gt;</code>
					</td>
					<td>Project root.</td>
				</tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>

		<h2>How it works</h2>
		<ol>
			<li>
				<strong>Codegen, exactly as usual.</strong> The same{" "}
				<code>.ayjnt/</code> tree that <code>ayjnt build</code> writes.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Real wrangler bundles the worker.</strong> ayjnt shells
				out to <code>wrangler deploy --dry-run --outdir</code> while{" "}
				<code>node_modules</code> is still available, so the bundle gets
				wrangler's own esbuild plus the{" "}
				<code>@cloudflare/unenv-preset</code>{" "}
				<code>nodejs_compat</code> treatment.{" "}
				<strong>The framework never bundles worker code itself</strong> —
				that keeps bundling fidelity out of the risk surface entirely.
				Whatever wrangler would deploy is what gets embedded.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>
					<code>bun build --compile</code> embeds everything:
				</strong>{" "}
				the worker script, the assets tree, <code>cli.ts</code>, every{" "}
				<code>tools.host.ts</code>, and (by default) the workerd binary.
			</li>
		</ol>

		<h3>Why the workerd module gets aliased</h3>
		<p>
			<code>miniflare</code> does a top-level{" "}
			<code>require("workerd")</code>, and that package resolves its native
			binary through <code>require.resolve</code>{" "}
			<em>at module-load time</em>. Inside a compiled binary there is no{" "}
			<code>node_modules</code>, so the import throws{" "}
			<code>Cannot find package 'workerd'</code> before miniflare's own
			override is ever consulted.
		</p>
		<Callout kind="note" title="MINIFLARE_WORKERD_PATH is not enough">
			<p>
				The env var is read too late — the module has already failed to
				load. <code>ayjnt compile</code> aliases the{" "}
				<code>workerd</code> module to a generated stub that reports the
				path the bootstrap extracted to. This is the main reason to use{" "}
				<code>ayjnt compile</code> rather than hand-rolling a{" "}
				<code>bun build --compile</code>.
			</p>
		</Callout>
		<p>
			A second, smaller reason: <code>import()</code> can't reach into a
			binary, so anything loaded dynamically —{" "}
			<code>cli.ts</code>, <code>tools.host.ts</code> — has to be imported{" "}
			<em>statically</em> by a generated bootstrap for Bun's bundler to see
			and include it.
		</p>

		<h2>What happens when you run it</h2>
		<p>
			On first launch the binary extracts workerd into a per-app cache
			directory, keyed by version. That's a ~103MB write, and it happens
			once — later runs find it and skip straight through. After that,
			startup is comfortably under a second. Then the binary calls the same{" "}
			<code>runApp</code> that{" "}
			<a href="/docs/guides/cli-file" class="link-underline">
				<code>ayjnt run</code>
			</a>{" "}
			does, with inputs read out of the executable instead of off disk.
		</p>
		<p>
			The binary claims only four flags for itself. Everything else becomes{" "}
			<code>argv</code> for your <code>cli.ts</code>:
		</p>
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr>
					<th>Flag</th>
					<th>Effect</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr>
					<td>
						<code>--port &lt;n&gt;</code>
					</td>
					<td>
						Port to bind. Default <code>8787</code>.
					</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>
						<code>--data-dir &lt;path&gt;</code>
					</td>
					<td>
						Where Durable Object state is persisted.
					</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>
						<code>--allow-host-writes</code>
					</td>
					<td>
						Permit host tools declaring{" "}
						<code>sideEffects: "write"</code>.
					</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>
						<code>--allow-host-exec</code>
					</td>
					<td>
						Permit host tools declaring{" "}
						<code>sideEffects: "exec"</code>.
					</td>
				</tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
		<p>
			If your <code>cli.ts</code> wants a flag of the same name, put it after
			an explicit <code>--</code> and the whole remainder is handed through
			untouched.
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			lang="sh"
			code={`./notes --port 9000 add "hello"     # --port is the app's; the rest is argv
./notes --allow-host-writes tool appendToLog '{"line":"hi"}'
./notes list`}
		/>

		<h2>Where state lives</h2>
		<p>
			A compiled ayjnt app is a shipped program, not a dev server, so
			Durable Object state has to outlive a single run and survive the
			binary being moved. That rules out both{" "}
			<code>.wrangler/state</code> (tied to a project checkout) and a temp
			directory. State goes in a per-app OS state directory, keyed by worker
			name so two apps never share storage:
		</p>
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr>
					<th>Platform</th>
					<th>Location</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr>
					<td>macOS</td>
					<td>
						<code>~/Library/Application Support/ayjnt/&lt;worker&gt;</code>
					</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>Linux</td>
					<td>
						<code>$XDG_STATE_HOME/ayjnt/&lt;worker&gt;</code>, else{" "}
						<code>~/.local/state/ayjnt/&lt;worker&gt;</code>
					</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>Windows</td>
					<td>
						<code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\ayjnt\&lt;worker&gt;</code>
					</td>
				</tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
		<p>
			Override it with <code>--data-dir &lt;path&gt;</code> or{" "}
			<code>AYJNT_DATA_DIR</code>; both <code>ayjnt run</code> and the
			compiled binary accept either. State survives across runs and across
			moving the binary, which is what makes{" "}
			<code>./notes add</code> today and <code>./notes list</code> tomorrow
			behave the way a user expects.
		</p>

		<h2>Limitations</h2>
		<Callout kind="warn" title="Four things to know before you ship a binary">
			<p>
				<strong>Browser tools don't work.</strong>{" "}
				<a href="/docs/guides/browser" class="link-underline">
					<code>ayjnt/browser</code>
				</a>{" "}
				needs a <code>worker_loaders</code> binding, and Miniflare has no
				equivalent — so browser tools fail in a compiled binary. Compile
				warns about it at build time. Everything else in the app still
				works; use <code>ayjnt dev</code> or a deployed worker when you
				need browser tools.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>
					Workers AI, Browser Rendering, and email sending are remote
					services.
				</strong>{" "}
				The binary is self-contained <em>as a runtime</em>, but these
				still need network access and Cloudflare credentials. Compile
				warns about whichever of them your config uses.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Cross-compiling needs the target's workerd.</strong>{" "}
				<code>--target</code> swaps Bun's runtime, but the embedded
				workerd is a native binary that must match the{" "}
				<em>target</em> platform. Only the host's{" "}
				<code>@cloudflare/workerd-&lt;platform&gt;</code> is installed by
				default, so compile fails with install instructions rather than
				embedding the wrong binary — which would build cleanly and die on
				first launch.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>macOS distribution needs codesigning</strong> — for both
				the outer binary and the workerd it extracts.
			</p>
		</Callout>
		<CodeBlock
			lang="sh"
			code={`# Cross-compiling for Linux from a Mac:
bun add -d @cloudflare/workerd-linux-64
ayjnt compile --target bun-linux-x64`}
		/>

		<h2>compile vs deploy</h2>
		<p>
			Same agents, two different targets — and the capability sets are not
			nested the way you might guess.
		</p>
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr>
					<th></th>
					<th>
						<code>ayjnt deploy</code>
					</th>
					<th>
						<code>ayjnt compile</code>
					</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr>
					<td>Where it runs</td>
					<td>Cloudflare's edge</td>
					<td>The user's machine</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>
						Host tools (<code>tools.host.ts</code>)
					</td>
					<td>No — refuses to deploy</td>
					<td>Yes</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>
						Local files, subprocesses, native{" "}
						<code>bun:sqlite</code>
					</td>
					<td>No</td>
					<td>Yes</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>
						Browser tools (<code>ayjnt/browser</code>)
					</td>
					<td>Yes</td>
					<td>No</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>Scale, TLS, global routing</td>
					<td>Cloudflare's</td>
					<td>Yours</td>
				</tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
		<p>
			<code>ayjnt deploy</code> ships to the edge and cannot run host
			tools, because there is no host process out there.{" "}
			<code>ayjnt compile</code> produces a local-first app with{" "}
			<strong>strictly more capability than Cloudflare</strong> for
			everything that touches the machine: local files, subprocesses,
			native SQLite. Pick per artifact, not per project — the same{" "}
			<code>agents/</code> tree can be both, as long as the agents tolerate
			a smaller ToolSet when deployed. See{" "}
			<a href="/docs/guides/agent-tools" class="link-underline">
				Agent tools
			</a>{" "}
			for the <code>@ayjnt-optional-on-deploy</code> marker that makes that
			explicit.
		</p>
	</DocPageShell>
</DocsLayout>
